# TypeScript conventions

## Context

`app/tsconfig.json` runs strict TS with `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, and every
existing module in `app/src/` uses named exports and `.js` extensions on
relative imports (ESM, `"type": "module"`). Code that ignores this compiles
locally in an agent's head but fails `npm run typecheck`.

## Rule

- Named exports only. NEVER `export default`.
- Relative imports carry the `.js` extension, and type-only imports use
  `import type`: `import type { Action, AppState } from "./types.js";`
- NEVER use `any`, `as any`, `@ts-ignore`, or `@ts-expect-error` to silence the
  compiler. Narrow the type, add the union variant, or widen the signature
  honestly.
- `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` is on: `state.tasks[0]` is `Task | undefined`.
  Guard it, or use `.find()`/`.at()` with an explicit check — do not assert
  with `!`.
- Immutable updates only: `{ ...state, tasks: state.tasks.map(...) }`. NEVER
  `push`, `splice`, `sort` in place, or assign to a property of existing state.
- File names kebab-case (`text.ts`, `ab-validation.md`); types and interfaces
  PascalCase; functions and variables camelCase.
- Comments explain *why*, not *what*. Match the density of the surrounding file
  — do not narrate every line.

## How to verify

1. `grep -rnE "\bany\b|@ts-ignore|@ts-expect-error|export default" app/src`
   — zero hits.
2. `grep -rnE "\.(push|splice|unshift|pop)\(|state\.[a-z]+ =" app/src/reducer.ts`
   — zero hits.
3. `cd app && npm run typecheck` — clean, with no suppressions added.
4. Every new `.ts` file's exports are importable by name from a test.
